<article class="env-remark" id="02L1">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02L1">Remark <span data-tag="02L1">3.1.5.12</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a locally Kan simplicial category (Definition <a data-tag="00JY" href="/tag/00JY">2.4.1.8</a>). Then the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ of Construction <a data-tag="00LX" href="/tag/00LX">2.4.6.1</a> inherits the structure of an $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$-enriched category, which can be described concretely as follows: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
For every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the mapping object $\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} }( X,Y )$ is the Kan complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)_{\bullet }$, regarded as an object of $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every pair of objects $X,Y,Z \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the composition law 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} }( Y,Z ) \times \underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} }( X,Y ) \rightarrow \underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} }( X,Z ) \]
</div>
<p>
 is the homotopy class of the composition map $\circ : \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(Y,Z)_{\bullet } \times \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)_{\bullet } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Z)_{\bullet }$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
